+(duration)=
+
+### Duration
+
+The {class}`Duration` type converts a duration into a {class}`datetime.timedelta`. A duration is either a whole number of
+seconds, like `120`, or one or more groups of an integer followed by a unit: `d` for days, `h` for hours, `m` for
+minutes, and `s` for seconds. Each unit may appear at most once and units must be in that order, with no spaces in
+between. Units are case-insensitive, so `2d`, `1h30m`, `45S`, `1d2h3m4s`, and `90m` are all valid. Negative numbers
+and decimals are not accepted. A {class}`datetime.timedelta` value, such as a `default`, is passed through unchanged.
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option("--timeout", type=click.Duration(), default="30s")
+    def wait(timeout):
+        click.echo(f"Waiting {timeout.total_seconds():g} seconds.")
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1h30m'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=120'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1.5h'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--help'])
+```
+
